END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Meetup: History of Parkdale
UID:event_316868607@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20261017T041428Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261107T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261107T160000
SUMMARY:The U of T\, Yorkville and Rochdale College (MEGA-TOUR)
DESCRIPTION:Toronto History Walks\n\\*\\***The Toronto History Walks Explo
 rer Pass is available. Get it now for $125.00 for 1 year of unlimited tour
 s\, email**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n**for more information or 
 for signing up!\\*\\***\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the tic
 ket price when booking your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to the
  following email at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n\n**This is a r
 evised route for 2026 to take in the history of the historic Rochdale Coll
 ege!**\n\nOn March 15\, 1827\, a royal charter was issued by King George I
 V\, for the establishment of King's College. The charter was the result of
  the efforts of John Strachan\, the Anglican Bishop of Toronto\, the Colle
 ge's first president.\n\nKing's College was a religious institution tied t
 o the Church of England and the Family Compact. In 1849\, the newly electe
 d government of Canada voted to rename the College\, University of Toronto
  and broke with the church. Strachan resigned to open Trinity College as a
  private seminary.\n\nUniversity College was created as a branch of Univer
 sity of Toronto. During the American Civil War\, the threat of Union block
 ade prompted the creation of the University Rifle Corps\, which saw action
  against the Fenians on the Niagara border in 1866.\n\nFounded in 1830 by 
 Joseph Bloor\, and William Botsford Jarvis\, the Village of Yorkville was 
 muddy York's northern residential suburb.\n\nWith historic establishments 
 like The Red Lion Inn\, Yorkville Town Hall or even the fierce political r
 adical William Lyon Mackenzie\, it was here that notoriety was achieved\, 
 in Yorkville itself.\n\nOmnibus horse drawn streetcar service started in 1
 849 to connect the old St. Lawrence Market in Toronto with Yorkville\, and
  by 1853\, the population of the village had reached 1\,000 residents.\n\n
 By the 1870s\, Potter's Field\, a cemetery stretching west of Yonge Street
  at Bloor Street was closed\, and the remains moved to other area cemeteri
 es. Yorkville was growing fast\, and soon Toronto was ready to pull the ne
 ighbourhood into its orbit. On February 1\, 1883\, Yorkville's name change
 d officially. It was now known as St. Paul's Ward!\n\nThis walking tour is
  for all fitness levels and skills. Please dress for the weather. The exte
 nsive walking tour begins at the Queen's Park SUBWAY Station at the **sout
 hwest corner** of University Avenue and College Street adjacent to the Tim
  Horton's at the corner.\n\nThe walk ends at the site of Historic Rochdale
  College at Bloor and Huron!\n\nThis tour has a ticket price of $15.00 per
  person!\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the ticket price when 
 booking your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to the following emai
 l at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/the-history-of-parkdale/events/316868
 607/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20261006T140328Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20261006T140328Z
CLASS:PUBLIC
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Meetup: History of Parkdale
UID:event_316868340@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20261017T041428Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261108T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261108T150000
SUMMARY:Landmarks 6: Gladstone Hotel to The Edgewater Hotel Via Gutta Perc
 ha
DESCRIPTION:Toronto History Walks\n**The Toronto History Walks Yearly Pass
  is available. Get it now for $125.00 for 1 year of unlimited tours\, emai
 l**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n**for more information or for sign
 ing up!**\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the ticket amount whe
 n booking your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to the following em
 ail at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n\nToronto is a city that hid
 es its history in plain sight. Beneath the glass towers are layers of stor
 ies — grand hotels that vanished overnight\, political scandals whispere
 d through stone corridors\, theatres that once glittered under gaslight\, 
 and laneways where the city first learned how to become a metropolis.\n\nT
 his new Toronto History Walks tour invites the walker to slow down\, and s
 ee the city differently.\n\nLet's return to a bygone era: late 1930s. Park
 dale would already have had a long fascinating history behind it\, but you
  would also be looking at a neighbourhood that was changing with the times
 . One of the most interesting examples was the Edgewater Hotel\, built in 
 1939 at 14 Roncesvalles Avenue\, at The Queensway.\n\nIt was built by Fred
  Hammer and became part of a very busy transportation hub\, sitting beside
  the Sunnyside Bus Terminal. The building has survived\, although the Edge
 water name eventually disappeared and the hotel went through several incar
 nations. It is a wonderful reminder that Parkdale's history isn't just Vic
 torian\, there is twentieth-century history here as well.\n\nAnd then we c
 ome to the Gladstone Hotel\, which is one of those buildings that almost s
 eem to have been watching the neighbourhood change. The hotel we see today
  dates from 1889 and was designed by Toronto architect George Martell Mill
 er in the Richardsonian Romanesque style. Its location was absolutely perf
 ect for business: it stood across from the Parkdale railway station\, and 
 was close to the Exhibition grounds. Travelling salesmen\, railway workers
 \, visitors to the CNE and commercial travellers could all find shelter he
 re.\n\nThe original owner was Susanna Robinson\, who was left a widow with
  thirteen children before construction of the hotel was completed.\nThe Gl
 adstone tells us something important about Parkdale itself. For decades it
  was a working-class gathering place\, and as the factories and industries
  changed\, so did the fortunes of the hotel. The great tower once had a cu
 pola that could be seen from a distance\, but that disappeared in the 1940
 s. By the later twentieth century the hotel had fallen on hard times and h
 ad developed a much different reputation. Yet it never completely lost its
  role as a community gathering place.\n\nEventually it was restored and re
 invented\, becoming part of the artistic and cultural life of Queen Street
  West.\n\nAnd finally\, we get to one of the great industrial stories of o
 ld Parkdale — the Gutta Percha & Rubber Manufacturing Company. Establish
 ed here in 1883 on West Lodge Avenue\, the factory became one of the large
 st employers in the neighbourhood and\, at its height\, the largest rubber
  manufacturer in Canada. They made all sorts of rubber products — hose\,
  belting\, boots\, footwear\, mats and eventually products connected with 
 the rapidly growing automobile industry.\n\nThe factory operated until 196
 0\, when changing technology\, synthetic rubber and competition finally br
 ought its long Parkdale story to an end. Three places — the Edgewater\, 
 the Gladstone and Gutta Percha — give us three very different Parkdales:
  the Parkdale of travel and transportation\, the Parkdale of hotels and co
 mmunity life\, and the Parkdale of factories and working people. Put them 
 together and you begin to see just how fascinating this neighbourhood real
 ly is.\n\nThis walking tour begins at The Gladstone Hotel at the northeast
  corner of Queen Street West and Gladstone Avenue\, one block east of Duff
 erin Street. We end at the corner of Queen Street West and Roncesvalles Av
 enue.\n\nThis walking tour is for all walking levels. Please dress for the
  season\, and hope to see you there.\n\nThis tour has a ticket price of $1
 5.00 per person.\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the ticket amo
 unt when booking your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to the follo
 wing email at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/the-history-of-parkdale/events/316868
 340/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20261006T133404Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20261006T133404Z
CLASS:PUBLIC
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Meetup: History of Parkdale
UID:event_315269045@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20261017T041428Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261114T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261114T140000
SUMMARY:The Battle of Lundy's Lane (LECTURE ON THE GO)
DESCRIPTION:Toronto History Walks\n**The Toronto History Walks Explorer Pa
 ss is available. Get it now for $125.00 for 1 year of unlimited tours\, em
 ail**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n**for more information or for si
 gning up!**\n\nWe have an amazing "Lecture on the Go" series for 2026-2027
 . New topics and fascinating stories abound! Join me\, and uncover the wor
 ld and keep active\, warm and cool during the winter and summer months!\n\
 nI started this walking classroom when we were under that horrible cloud c
 alled Covid. This series was popular and helped people get back into socie
 ty and life.\n\nThe "Lecture on the Go" Series is open to members who have
  purchased the YEARLY MEMBERSHIP PASS\, it's free for those members. If yo
 u wish to attend a lecture and you are not a yearly pass member then the p
 rice **per** lecture is $20.00.\n\nThe Battle of Lundy’s Lane was one of
  the largest and bloodiest battles fought during the War of 1812. It took 
 place on July 25\, 1814\, near Niagara Falls. American forces had crossed 
 into Upper Canada (Ontario) and were pushing north along the Niagara Penin
 sula. British troops\, Canadian militia\, and Indigenous allies moved to s
 top them\, and the two armies met near a small country lane known as Lundy
 ’s Lane.\n\nThe fierce battle lasted well into the night\, with soldiers
  often fighting in darkness\, and confusion.\nOne of the most important po
 ints in the battle was a hill where British artillery had been placed. Bot
 h sides recognized its importance and launched repeated attacks. It was in
 credibly intense\, with troops charging\, and retreating\, again and again
 . Officers on both sides were wounded\, and casualties mounted. By the tim
 e the battle ended\, neither army had achieved a clear victory\, but the A
 mericans eventually withdrew from the area\, leaving the British in contro
 l of the field. It was a costly struggle\, and thousands were killed or mi
 ssing.\n\nToday\, the Battle of Lundy’s Lane is remembered as a turning 
 point in the Niagara campaign and one of the defining battles of the war. 
 The battle demonstrated the determination of the Canadians who fought to d
 efend this country.\n\nAlthough the war itself ended without major territo
 rial changes\, Lundy’s Lane became an important symbol of resilience. Vi
 sitors can still explore the historic battlefield in Niagara Falls and lea
 rn about the dramatic events that unfolded there more than two centuries a
 go\, and made the country we call Canada. If you like history and Canadian
  history at that\, or want to learn so much more then this lecture is for 
 you.\n\nUpcoming topics in the 2026 series:\n\n\\*Philosophy 1: Great Mind
 s of the Western World: Goethe to Kant! - (December 2026)\n\\*Philosophy 2
 : Great Minds of the Eastern\nWorld: Chuang Tzu to Dalai Lama - (December 
 2026)\nLegacy of Triangle: How a New York Factory Scandal changed North Am
 erica - (January 2027)\n\nSo book your plans now!\n\nThis event is for all
  levels and walkers\, there are a few steps here and there to navigate\, b
 ut mostly on one level. This event in a **non-walking** event.\n\nThe lect
 ure begins on the steps of **Old City Hall** at the northeast corner of Ba
 y Street and Queen Street West\, and\nhas a ticket price of $20.00 per per
 son\, but **free with the yearly membership pass**.\n\n**Please note: Plea
 se e-transfer the ticket amount when booking your space for this tour! You
  can e-transfer to the following email at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.
 com**
